How can I design an adaptive user interface using HTML and CSS?
In order to create an adaptive user interface, you can use media queries in CSS to adjust the layout and design of your web page based on the user's device and screen size. 
Here is an example of how you can use media queries in CSS:
```
/* Styles for desktop devices */
@media only screen and (min-width: 992px) {
  /* Desktop styles here */
}
/* Styles for tablet devices */
@media only screen and (min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 991px) {
  /* Tablet styles here */
}
/* Styles for mobile devices */
@media only screen and (max-width: 767px) {
  /* Mobile styles here */
}
```
By using these media queries, you can create different styles for different devices and screen sizes, making your user interface more adaptive and user-friendly.

reference_answers.high_quality:
An adaptive user interface (UI) is a UI that automatically adjusts its layout and design based on the user's device and screen size. This is done using media queries in CSS.

Media queries are a CSS feature that allows you to target specific devices and screen sizes with your CSS styles. You can use media queries to make your website look good on all devices, from small mobile phones to large desktop monitors.

To create an adaptive UI, you need to identify the different devices and screen sizes that your website will be viewed on. Then, you need to create different CSS styles for each device and screen size. You can use media queries to target each device and screen size, and then apply the appropriate CSS styles.

Here is an example of how you can use media queries to create an adaptive UI:

```css
/* Styles for desktop devices */
@media only screen and (min-width: 992px) {
  /* Desktop styles here */
}

/* Styles for tablet devices */
@media only screen and (min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 991px) {
  /* Tablet styles here */
}

/* Styles for mobile devices */
@media only screen and (max-width: 767px) {
  /* Mobile styles here */
}
```

In this example, we are targeting three different devices: desktop computers, tablets, and mobile phones. We are using media queries to apply different CSS styles to each device. For example, we are applying desktop styles to devices with a minimum width of 992px, tablet styles to devices with a minimum width of 768px and a maximum width of 991px, and mobile styles to devices with a maximum width of 767px.

You can use media queries to target any device or screen size. You can also use media queries to target specific features of devices, such as the orientation of the screen or the presence of a touch screen.

By using media queries, you can create an adaptive UI that looks good on all devices and is easy to use for all users.

