    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-3">Column 1</div>
        <div class="col-3">Column 2</div>
        <div class="col-3">Column 3</div>
    </div>

</body>
</html>
```

The "viewport" meta tag in the head section of your HTML helps ensure your web page is scaled and rendered properly on different devices. The flexible grid makes use of percentage widths for columns and adapts to different screen sizes.

2. Using CSS media queries to adjust the layout based on screen size:

```css
/* For mobile devices */
@media only screen and (max-width: 600px) {
    [class*="col-"] {
        width: 100%;
    }
}

/* For tablets */
@media only screen and (min-width: 601px) and (max-width: 992px) {
    .col-1 {width: 100%;}
    .col-2 {width: 50%;}
    .col-3 {width: 33.33%;}
    // Add more column widths as needed
}

/* For desktops */
@media only screen and (min-width: 993px) {
    .col-1 {width: 8.33%;}
    .col-2 {width: 16.66%;}
    .col-3 {width: 25%;}
    // Add more column widths as needed
}
```

CSS media queries allow you to adjust the layout of your webpage based on the screen size or device type. You can adjust the column widths, font sizes, and other styles based on the screen size by using the `@media` rule.

Best practices for optimizing performance and minimizing load times:

1. Minify CSS and JavaScript: Remove unnecessary spaces, comments, and characters to reduce file size and improve load times.

2. Optimize images: Use the proper format (JPEG for photographs and PNG for graphics) and compress images to reduce file size.

3. Use CDN (Content Delivery Network): It serves static resources like images, stylesheets, and JavaScript files from a server geographically closer to the user, improving load times.

4. Lazy loading: Load images and other resources only when the user scrolls to the specific section of the page.

5. Browser caching: Utilize browser caching to store static resources like images, stylesheets, and JavaScript files in the user's browser, reducing load times for subsequent visits.

6. Keep the number of HTTP requests to a minimum: Reducing the number of requests for external resources can improve the initial load time of your webpage.

Following these best practices, along with creating a responsive design using HTML, CSS, and media queries, will ensure a fast-loading and visually appealing webpage regardless of the device or screen size used to access it.

