To find the first five instances of the keyword 'Android' on www.example.com and exclude any instances found within HTML tags, you can use PHP and the DOMDocument with DOMXPath classes to parse the HTML and search through its text nodes. Here is a sample PHP code to achieve this:

```php
function getTextBetweenTags($string, $tagname) {
    $doc = new DOMDocument();
    $doc->loadHTML($string);
    $tags = $doc->getElementsByTagName($tagname);
    $texts = [];
    foreach ($tags as $tag) {
        $texts[] = $tag->nodeValue;
    }
    return $texts;
}

$url = "http://www.example.com";
$content = file_get_contents($url);
$textContent = getTextBetweenTags($content, 'body');
$text = implode(' ', $textContent);

$keyword = "Android";
$count = 0;
$max_instances = 5;
$offset = 0;
$positions = [];

while (($pos = mb_stripos($text, $keyword, $offset)) !== false && $count < $max_instances) {
    $positions[] = $pos;
    $offset = $pos + mb_strlen($keyword);
    $count++;
}

echo "The first $max_instances instances of '$keyword' can be found at the following positions in the text content:\n";
print_r($positions);
```

In the above code, we first create a function called `getTextBetweenTags`, which parses an HTML string and returns an array of text nodes between the specified HTML tags (in this case, the 'body' tag). Then, we loop over each text node, and using `mb_stripos` with increasing `$offset`, we look for the first five instances of the 'Android' keyword.

This approach filters out any instances of the keyword found within HTML tags by only looking at the text content. The code is efficient, as it uses DOM parsing to extract text nodes from the HTML, and then scans the text content for the keyword.

An alternative to using DOMDocument and DOMXPath could be using a third-party PHP HTML parser like SimpleHtmlDomParser to achieve a similar result. But the provided solution should be efficient and meet the requirements in your question.

